Dogen was a Zen priest in the early Kamakura period.
He was the founder of the Soto sect.
In his later years, he was also called Maregen.
It is also called Takoso in the same sect.
His shigo (a posthumous name) was Busshodenshi and Joyo Daishi.
It is generally called Dogen Zenji.
It is said that it spread the custom of grinding teeth to Japan, and when eating it, it spread the customs of cleaning and cleaning.
There is also a theory that he first brought back Momoo chiku (Moshitake) (Moshitake).
Although it is not known when Dogen was born, there are various theories that he was born as the direct descendant of Naidaijin Michichika TSUCHIMIKADO (MINAMOTO no Michichika or Michichika KOGA).
It is said that he was born as the son of Michichika and FUJIWARA no Koreko, the daughter of Daijo-daijin (Grand minister of state) Motofusa MATSUDONO (FUJIWARA no Motofusa) in Matsudono villa in Kyoto, but recent studies have become widely accepted that he was the biological son of Michitomo HORIKAWA, who is said to be his foster father.
There is also a theory that Michichika's son, MINAMOTO no Michimune or Michimitsu KOGA, was the father of Michichika.
According to the biography "Kenkoki," he lost his father (Michichika) at the age of three, and was adopted by Michitomo HORIKAWA, his older paternal half-brother, when he was eight years old.
According to one theory, there was a story that after the death of his maternal uncle, Moroie MATSUDONO (the former Sessho Naidaijin (Minister of the Center)), who wanted to succeed to the Matsudono family, but it is said that Dogen, who felt unnecessary of his life, refused.
There is a theory that Shinran, the founder of Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism), had a close relationship with each other, but there is no evidence that he had a relationship with each other.
There is a theory that the chapter of "Shobogenzo" in his book "Shobogenzo" was written by Shinran.
In 1213, he visited Yoshiaki, his maternal uncle of Mt. Hiei.
In 1214, he entered the priesthood under Kinen, the Tendai-zasu (head priest of the Tendai sect), and called himself Hobo Domoto.
In 1215, he studied Tendai Sect under Kintane of Onjo-ji Temple.
In 1217, he studied under Myozen, a disciple of Eisai at Kennin-ji Temple.
In 1223, along with Myozen, he went to the Southern Sung Dynasty from Hakata to the Southern Sung Dynasty, and received an Inka from Joko, a Buddhist monk of the Soto sect.
He returned to Japan in 1228.
In 1233, he founded Kosho-ji Temple in Fukakusa, Kyoto.
Around this time, he was oppressed from Mt. Hiei.
In July 1243, he was invited by Yoshishige HATANO, jito (manager and lord of manor) of Echizen Province, and moved to Shibisho, Echizen Province.
On the way, he responded to the invitation of Nobutsuna SASAKI, a feudal lord of Kutsuki-mura Village, and went to the Kutsuki-mura Village (the origin of Kosho-ji Temple (Takashima City)
In 1244, he founded Daibutsu-ji Temple in Kasamatsu.
In 1246, Daibutsu-ji Temple changed its name to Eihei-ji Temple, and its go (pen name) was Maregen.
Around this time, he went down to Kamakura to teach Buddhism by the invitation of the regent Tokiyori HOJO and Yoshishige HATANO.
It was a half year period of training in Kamakura, but it became the beginning of pure Zen Buddhism in the Kanto region.
In 1253, he handed over Eihei-ji Temple to his disciple Ejo Koun, and died at the residence of Kakuen (Takatsuji-dori Nishinotoin-dori Street, Kyoto City) at the age of 54.
The cause of his death is said to be ulcer.
