Umekoji Steam Locomotive Museum is a steam locomotive designated by West Japan Railway Company (JR West) in Kankiji-cho, Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
On October 10, 1972, Japan National Railways used the Sensu in Umekoji Ward, Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the establishment of Japan Railway Company's establishment, and it was the first facility for the Japan Railway Company as a valuable cultural property and the first facility for the purpose of preservation of steam locomotives.
With the division and privatization of JNR in 1987, JR West took over.
This facility itself is the site of the current train-cars (Umekoji Operation Ward), and is also carried out and repairs and repairs of the steam locomotives owned by the Sagano Sight Steam Locomotive Railway Company.
In the first place, this facility became the beginning of the 1960's, because steam locomotives disappeared rapidly from the latter half of the 1960s.
At the beginning, the Koyama Organization, which was located near the capital area, was the most important candidate for the Preservation Organization, but from the viewpoint of 'the center of Japan' and 'the former site of a historic site' in 1970, the Umekoji Office (at that time) was officially selected as the preservation institutions.
In principle, the cars for preservation were considered to be selected for the first-class train cars (in principle, the first-class train-cars) but there were some cars that were originally scheduled to be preserved at the beginning like C62 model No.22.
The facility consists of the "Source: Steam Locomotive Museum" which used the Sensu in Umekoji Ward, and the former Nijo Station building was relocated and restored.
The Sensu-kuri was a reinforced concrete structure built in 1914, and was designated as a national important cultural property along with the hikikomi Line on December 10, 2004.
In the same year, in 2004, he was selected as a member of the Institute for Chemistry.
In 2006, the former Nijo Station building (an exhibition hall) and the Sensu-shako Depot, which are preserved and preserved by JR West, were designated as a National Monuments for the Railway's monuments.
The former Nijo Station building was constructed in 1904 by the oldest wooden building of the Japan Railway Co., Ltd., which was built as the head office of the company, and the oldest wooden building of the Japanese-style building, which was built in consideration of the scenery of Heian-jingu Shrine.
The Kyoto Railway was nationalized in 1907 and then used as the station building of JR West, but in 1996, the station building ended as an elevated station building of the Sanin Main Line (Sagano Line) and Hanazono Station (Kyoto Prefecture) and was restored in 1997 and used as a site of the main building of the main building and used as an important materials.
It was designated as a cultural property of Kyoto City in April 1996.
In Sensukuri, the 16-yen steam locomotives produced from the Taisho period to the Showa period (at the beginning of the opening, 16-yen) are installed and exhibits and exhibits.
At the beginning of the opening, there were 15 cars of the C53 type C53 and 239 cars of C51 and 2299 cars of C59 cars, but since then, the number of cars for the preservation is 77, seven cars have been preserved for the preservation.
The five-car train cars still have a car, and two-car trains (C571/C56/C56/C56) are offered to trains on the Tokaido Main Line, such as 'Yamagachi (train)' on the Hokuriku Main Line and 'Kitabiko (train)' on the Hokuriku Main Line.
Although there are two motorcycles other than these two cars, it's impossible to run in front of the main line because they don't have any examinations.
In addition, the 'SL Square' was operated by the track of the building for the purpose of preserving the inside of the museum.
This exhibition line was once extended to the north of the station building, which is now a large parking lot (formerly a park and park), but the station building of the former Nijo Station building was moved to the south end of the Sagano Line, and the station building was relocated to the south end of the present Umekoji-koen Park.
Even today, it has its function as an organization, and is connected with the business line.
