Shomyo is one of Japanese traditional music.
It is a religious music used for rites and festivals.
In Japan, it is also called Bonbai or Bonno-ku.
According to the old Chinese characters, it was written as "seimyo" in the old Chinese characters.
It is one of the academic fields of ancient India.
Gomyo refers to five kinds of learning (the study of phonology), Shomyo (the study of art and techniques), Kenmei (the study of art and technology), Inmei (the study of medicine), Inmei (the study of ethics), and Naimyo (the study of Buddhism in Buddhism) (in the case of Buddhist monks).
It has been handed down to Japan together with the introduction of Buddhism to Japan.
There is a record that a Buddhist memorial service was held in 754 when the Great Buddha statue of Todai-ji Temple was held, and it is believed that Shomyo was popular in the Nara period.
In the early Heian period, Saicho and Kukai introduced their voice and became the basis of Tendai Shomyo and Shingon Shomyo.
In addition to the Tendai sect and the Shingon sect, there is a unique voice of each sect, and it is still handed down to the present day.
In order to transmit Shomyo, there was no Rakufu.
Therefore, it was extremely difficult to learn the teachings.
Later, Hakase (a doctor) was invented.
There are differences in special terms such as hakase depending on each school.
However, Hakase was a reference to recitation, and if he tried to learn Shomyo, he needed kuden (also known as roi) (or junju) (or junju) and could not maintain and maintain the school from his master.
Therefore, the development of leaders and heirs was essential.
With various conditions, many schools were abolished.
The Tendai Shomyo was based on Saicho's teachings, and its original development was developed.
After Saicho, Ennin and Anzen flourished.
Ryonin, the founder of Yuzu Nenbutsu, is known as the restorer of Yuzu Nenbutsu.
In 1109, Ryonin built Raigo-in Temple (Sakyo Ward, Kyoto City) in Ohara, Kyoto.
The sango (literally,"mountain name"), which is the title of the Raigo-in Temple in Ohara, is called "Gyozan" (literally,"mountain name"), which is the place where Shomyo in China originated, is called "Gyozan."
Before long, two temples of Raigo-in Temple and Shorin-in Temple came to be known as a dojo (training hall) of Ohara-ryu Shomyo-ji Temple.
In addition, Jakuhara later became one of the schools, and in Ohara there was a voice of two schools.
Later, Sokai restored Daibara Shomyo.
Tanchi built a flow based on the new theory of music.
Since then, he had no center of Tendai Shomyo (Buddhist masses) and succeeded to the current Tendai Shomyo.
The Yuzu Nenbutsu sect, Jodo sect and Jodo Shinshu Shomyo are the lineage of Tendai Shomyo.
The Shingon Shomyo is based on Kukai's teachings, and it is still in existence today.
Shomyo has been systematized since the era of Shinga.
It can be said that he was the restorer of the Kancho era.
He worked for the creation and development of shomyo (a Japanese poem of thirty-one syllables).
There were many schools until the Kamakura period, but it was compiled by Cloistered Imperial Prince Kakusho (Honno-in school, Shinsoo-in school, Daigo-ryu school, and Nakagawa Daishin school).
The Shomyo of the Kogi Shingon sect declined and disappeared from the Edo period.
The Honsoo-in school, Shinsoo-in school and Daigo-ryu school were abolished until the middle of the Meiji period.
At present, it is divided into Chizan Shomyo (Kyoto Chishaku-in Temple), Buzan Shomyo (Buzan-ji Temple in Nara) and Nanzan Shinryu (Nanzan Shinryu school, Kyoto and Kogi Shingon sect temples).
Chizan Shomyo and Toyozan Shomyo (Shingi Shingon sect Shomyo), the Chizan school of the Shingon sect, the Buzan school of the Shingon sect, and the Shomyo of both schools originated from the Nakagawa Daijin school.
Raiyu adopted the old school of Daigo.
When Negoro-ji Temple (Wakayama Prefecture) was burned down by Hideyoshi TOYOTOMI in 1583, the two schools of Chizan and Toyoyama were established as old schools of Daigo.
It is called 'Ron-gi' or 'Shomyo' in Mt. Chizan.
Nanzan Shinryu school (Kogi Shingon sect Shomyo) became the origin of the Nakagawa Daijin school.
The Nakagawa-Oshin-ryu school was founded by Daishin of Nakagawa-dera Temple in Nara.
During the Joei era (1222-1233), Shoshin of Mt. Koya and Sanbo-in moved his base to Mt. Koya.
Later, the name of Mt. Koya was changed to Mt. Minami-san and was called Nanzan Shinryu.
It is also called Shinryu and Noyama shinryu.
It can be said that the songs such as Heikyoku, Yokyoku, Minyo and Joruri were changed to Shomyo.
It has a great influence on the melody music.
Emperor Tenchi (Tenchi)
A man.
626 - January 3, 672 (January 7, 672)
He was the 38th Emperor.
His Japanese-style posthumous name was Amenokoyanehiko no mikoto (also known as Akatsusakawake no mikoto).
His imina (personal name) was Kazuraki (Katsuragi).
It is believed that he was called Kazuraki no Miko (also known as Kazuragi no Miko) before his birth.
It is generally known as Naka no Oe no Oji or Naka no Oe no Miko.
'Oe' means the Crown Prince, and 'Naka no Oe' means 'the next Crown Prince.'
Summary
He was the second prince of Emperor Jomei.
His mother was Hokyo no Himemiko (later Emperor Kogyoku).
His Empress was Yamatohime no Okimi, a daughter of her older paternal half-brother, Furuhito no Oe no Oji.
He plotted a coup d'etat with NAKATOMI no Kamatari and killed SOGA no Iruka and made his uncle, Emperor Kotoku ascend the throne, and he himself became the Crown Prince.
He established the era name of the Taika era and carried out various reforms (the central figure of the Taika Reforms and the Isshi Incident).
Also, after Prince Arima, he was killed in a coup d'etat with a trap of power.
Since Baekje was destroyed by Tang and Silla in 660, he sent Buyeo Pung, the prince of Baekje who stayed in the Imperial Court, and tried to restore Baekje.
He stayed in Tsukushi in order to save Baekje, but died in 661.
After that, he did not take the imperial throne for a long time, but after the defeat in the Battle of Hakusukinoe in 663, he moved to Otsu City and ascended the throne.
After the Battle of Hakusukinoe, the castle was established as a part of the defense policy for the defense of the land.
In addition, the cap rank was also reformed from the 19th floor to the 26th floor of the system.
In 670, he made the oldest national family register 'Kogo no Nenjaku' (a family register of years) in Japan.
According to Nihonshoki (Chronicles of Japan), Emperor Tenchi had the first prince, Prince Otomo succeed to the throne.
However, in the Jinshin War which occurred after the death of Emperor Tenchi, his younger brother, Prince Oama (Emperor Tenmu) won Prince Otomo and ascended the throne.
Since then, the Emperor Tenmu line of Emperor Tenmu continued until Emperor Shotoku.
After the death of Shotoku, Shirakabe no Okimi, a grandson of Emperor Tenchi, ascended the throne as Emperor Konin, and after that, he became a line of Emperor Tenchi.
It is said that he captured Nukata no Okimi from Prince Oama and sent four daughters to Prince Oama as the wife of Prince Oama.
Brief Personal History
626 - He was born.
June 12, 645: He became Crown Prince.
Saimei July 24, 661 - Shosei (a system of centralized government based on the ritsuryo code)
He succeeded to the throne on February 20, 668.
January 7, 672 - Died at the age of 46.
