Yoshinobu TOKUGAWA (also known as Yoshihisa TOKUGAWA) was the 15th Seii Taishogun (literally,"great general who subdues the barbarians") of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) (reigned from December 5, 1866 to December 9, 1867).
He was the only shogun who did not perform the role of shogun in Edo-jo Castle.
He was Naidaijin (minister of the center) Juichii (Junior First Rank) and the Grand Cordon of the Order of the Merit.
He was a member of Kizokuin (the House of Peers).
After the death of the 14th Shogun Iemochi TOKUGAWA, who served as a guardian for the shogun, he assumed the position of the 15th shogun of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
After the Emperor Meiji returned the political power to the Imperial Court, he tried to overthrow the Imperial Court, but in the Battle of Toba-Fushimi after the Restoration of Imperial Rule (Japan), he returned to Edo-jo Castle after he left the army of the former bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) and was ordered by the Imperial Court to hunt down and kill the Imperial Court and surrender of Edo-jo Castle.
In the Meiji period, when he was released from confinement, he died in his hobbby, and he was born as a prince until the Taisho period.
Yoshinobu' is also read as 'Yoshinobu' or 'Keiki' (Yusoku-yomi).
In official documents of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), there is a record of 'Yoshihisa' in the official documents of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
It is also written as 'Yeshan' in the alphabet and the English newspaper.
It is often called 'Yoshinobu' in Mito City, where he was born, but it is often called 'Keki' in Shizuoka City where he spent his life.
