On August 1, 1847, the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) ordered the Mito Domain to be the heir of Shichiromaro to be the heir of the Hitotsubashi family.
In response to this, Shichiro Morimaro inherited the Hitotsubashi family on September 1, and in December, he was granted the name of Yoshinobu by the 12th shogun Ieyoshi TOKUGAWA and called himself Yoshinobu.
Ieyoshi often visited the Hitotsubashi residence, and he thought Yoshinobu would be an influential candidate for the heir of the shogun, but he was remonstrated by Masahiro ABE, roju (member of shogun's council of elders).
In 1853, during the chaos of the arrival of the Black Ships, Shogun Ieyoshi TOKUGAWA died of illness, and the 13th Shogun Iesada TOKUGAWA, who succeeded to the position of the head of the family, did not have a son, so the issue of 'the successor of the shogun' (the issue of the succession of the Shogun's heir).
