On July 6, the bakufu appointed Yoshinobu as Shogun's guardian and Shungaku as the president of political affairs.
Yoshinobu and Shungaku did not carry out the reform of the shogunate government called Bunkyu Reforms, and promoted the establishment of Kyoto Shugoshoku (Military governor of Kyoto) and the relaxation of Sankinkotai (a system under which feudal lords in the Edo period were required to spend every other year in residence in Edo) for Sankinkotai (a system under which feudal lords in the Edo period were required to spend every other year in residence in Edo).
In 1863, he went up to Kyoto as a pioneer of Shogun Iemochi TOKUGAWA, and worked hard to negotiate with the Imperial Court, which forced the expulsion of foreigners.
