Yoshinobu, who had been predicting 'the secret imperial decree to overthrow the shogunate' to Satsuma-Choshu (Satsuma Domain), submitted a return of government to the Imperial Court to the Imperial Court on October 14, 1867 (the Taisei Hokan (transfer of power back to the Emperor)).
It is said that the Imperial Court at that time had no administrative power, and tried to make the Tokugawa government survive in the form of the council of feudal lords.
However, Toshimichi OKUBO and Tomomi IWAKURA, who were trying to overthrow the Shogunate, issued the Decree for the Restoration of Imperial Rule in December, and Yoshinobu ordered 'Jikan nochi' (a place for resignation of Naidaijin and Tokugawa's territory).
Yoshinobu left Osaka-jo Castle to avoid conflict, and insisted on the legitimacy of the Tokugawa shogunate, the Imperial Court, and further modified the land of Jikan nochi.
However, in 1868, the following year, the Satsuma Domain raised an army in Edo, and the Satsuma Domain took up arms and closed Kyoto with soldiers of Aizu and Kuwana Domains.
However, in the Battle of Toba and Fushimi which broke out on January 3, the army of the former bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) in the Battle of Toba and Fushimi, the army of the former Shogunate had still maintained enough military power, and retreated to Edo with "Kaiyomaru" (Kaiyo-maru), which was a military epic.
Soon after that, the Imperial Court issued an order to hunt down and kill Yoshinobu, and the government army led by Imperial Prince Arisugawanomiya Taruhito started the expedition to the east.
