Nara Line is a railway line of West Japan Railway Company (JR West) that runs from Kizu Station (Kyoto Prefecture) in Kizu City, Kyoto Prefecture to Kyoto Station in Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
All lines are included in the suburbs of urban areas.
Because the line has a history of the Kansai Main Line, the official starting point is Kizu Station, but the train runs from Kyoto Station to Kizu Station (the number of trains on the train number of trains is unknown) and the opposite (the number of trains is the same).
Except for the data, there is no special mention in the following description, and it is described in the section from Kyoto to Kizu.
The color of the line is green (dark) and it is said that the reason for the selection is 'the image that the two old capital city is an old city.'
Management (railway business type): West Japan Railway Company (railway business operator)
Route distance (operation kilometers): 34.7 km
Track gauge: 1067 mm
Number of stations: 19 (including the starting stations)
Double-track section: between Shinden Station and Uji Station, JR Fujimori Station and Kyoto Station
Electrified section: The entire line (DC 1500V)
Block (railway): Automatic block system (track section), automatic block system (one-track section)
Traffic device: Automatic Train Stop
Drive for operation: Shin-Osaka General Steward
Maximum speed: 110 km/h (track section), 95 km/h (one-track section)
It is under the control of the Kyoto branch of the West Japan Railway Company (Kizu Station is operated by the West Japan Railway Company, Osaka Branch Office of the West Japan Railway Company).
The line between Kyoto and Kizu is named the Nara Line, but the entire section is located in Kyoto Prefecture and there is no line in Nara Prefecture.
This is due to the fact that the line between Kyoto and Nara was built by Nara Railway, but after the merger of Kansai Railway Company, the line was separated from Kizu and south of the Nagoya route as a part of the Osaka - Nagoya route.
However, trains run from Kizu Station to Nara Station on the Kansai Main Line (Yamatoji Line) in Nara Prefecture.
JR West established the name of the line (JR Kyoto Line and JR Kobe Line) on March 13, 1988 due to the revision of the Urban Network, however, the Nara Line was sent to the Nara Line.
Because Kintetsu Nara Line is also operated by Kintetsu Corporation, it is sometimes referred to as 'JR Nara Line.'
In the era of Japan National Railways, the Japanese National Railways (JNR) was described as 'the Nara Line of Japan National Railways,' and the English name of the guide board was also used as 'JNR.'
Kintetsu Nara Line is in conflict with the Yamatoji Line in Naniwa and Nara, and the Kintetsu Kyoto Line is the Kintetsu Kyoto Line, which is a competitive relationship between the Nara Line and the JR Nara Line.
While many trains on the Keihan Uji Line and Kintetsu Kyoto Line were operated in parallel with each other, the Nara Line was not electrified until 1984 at the end of the JNR era, and even after the electrification of JNR/JR Suburban Train Series 103 was limited to two-car trains of JNR/JR Suburban Train Series 103 and so on.
However, after JR West was established, the improvement of transportation rapidly increased due to the increase in the number of rapid trains such as 'Miyakoji Rapid' (some trains have six-car trains) and 'Miyakoji Rapid' (some trains have six-car trains) in the middle of the Urban Network.
Today, it has a strong nature as a commuter line from the southern part of Kyoto Prefecture, such as Byodo-in Temple in Uji City, which is located along the line, and as a commuter line from the southern part of Kyoto Prefecture.
However, the section between Momoyama Station and Shinden Station is a big detour on the east side, and in the south of Joyo Joyo, there is a difference between the Kintetsu Kyoto Line, which runs outside the central part of the town, and the section between Kyoinama and regional transportation.
The car is used for Nara Train Depot, which is operated by Osaka Branch Office, but the facilities for the station are managed by Kyoto Branch except Kizu Station.
J-Thru, ICOCA, Suica of East Japan Railway Company (JR East) and TOICA of Central Japan Railway Company (JR Central) can be used at all stations.
In the south of Joyo Station, there is a simple ticket gate to the south of Joyo Station.
Since the express 'Kuroshio (train)' operated between Kyoto Station and Wakayama Station via the Nara Line, Sakurai Line and Wakayama Line was abolished in 1984, only local trains were operated until 1991 (for a special express train, a special express train was operated at a special time as a limited express train, a type of local train, a type of local train, a type of local train, and a type of local train, a type of local train.
It is the fastest type of train on the Nara Line, and is mainly operated during the daytime.
