Higashi Hongan-ji Temple is a Buddhist temple belonging to Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) located in Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
It is the head temple of the Shinshu sect Otani school (Shinshu Jisshu sect) and is the head temple of the Jodo Shinshu sect (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) along with Nishi Hongan-ji Temple (the head temple of the Hongan-ji school of Jodo Shinshu sect (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) Hongan-ji Temple).
The official name of the temple was 'Hongan-ji Temple' until 1987, and after the same year, it was 'Shinshu Honbyo' (Shinshu Honbyo).
To distinguish it from Nishi Hongan-ji Temple in Shimogyo Ward, it is often called 'Oto-san.'
(See the article on the history of Hongan-ji Temple for details before the split into east and west.)
When Kyonyo (Koju), the 12th head of Hongan-ji Temple, was given the territory of the temple in the east of Hongan-ji Temple by Ieyasu TOKUGAWA in 1602, Kyonyo opened the temple in 1602 when Hongan-ji Temple was divided into two.
It is now located at Karasuma Shichijo, Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, and it is called Higashi Hongan-ji Temple because it is located in the east of Nishi Hongan-ji Temple located in Horikawa Shichijo, Kyoto City.
In 1987, Hongan-ji Temple as a religious corporation, which was related to the Otani school and the Bekukuri, was dissolved, and it was considered to be one of the religious schools.
After that, the official name of Higashi Hongan-ji Temple was 'Shinshu Honbyo' (the main mausoleum of the Shinshu sect) (honbyo' means the basic training hall and the devotion of Shinran, the founder of the sect, who heard the teachings of Shinran, and became a believer of Shinran).
It also means to distinguish it from the Higashi Hongan-ji school of Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) which separated from the Higashi Hongan-ji school.
otodo sodo (to the east of the city)
In the Edo period, many of the existing buildings were rebuilt during the Meiji period after the fire, and many of the existing buildings were rebuilt during the Meiji period after the fire.
Miei-do hall, which enshrines Shinran's statue, is the largest wooden building in the world (it is covered by the protective roof).
The Goei-do Gate is one of the three major gates of the Sanmon gate.
It is owned by Shinran's Shinseki-shinbun (a collection of real teachings of the Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) (a national treasure) (the Bando-bon).
Shoseien-en Garden, which is located near the ground, is a place of scenic beauty in Japan.
