In 1880 to 1881, a fierce dispute over the enshrined deity of Shinto Jimyo-den Hall, which was established in Hinodani, Tokyo, occurred in the Shinto world.
Shindo Jimukyoku (the Bureau of Shinto Shrines) insisted that the three gods (Tenno no Minakanushi no Kami, Takamimusubi no Kami, Kamimusubi no Kami, and Amaterasu Omikami) were enshrined in the Shinto shrine, but the 'Izumo-ha' (the Izumo school) should have 'Yukenyo' and 'Izumo-no-yo' (the one of the deities of the world) and 'Izumo-no-no-kami' (the god of the world) should be controlled by Okuninushi no Kami.
However, the 'Ise-ha' (Ise school), which was the center of the Shinto Jimukyoku, was 'Tenchi Daizaisho' (the head of heaven and earth), which Amaterasu Omikami ruled the two worlds, and the other Shinto priests insisted that they were not under the subject, and the other sects were opposed to each other.
