The term "Daimyo" refers to a word that was changed from daimyo (Japanese feudal lord) to daimyos (feudal lord).
It refers to the most important person.
It is a small name.
Originally, he told people who had power in the local regions.
In the samurai society, it came to mean samurai who owned many shoryo (territory) and subordinates.
In the Muromachi period, Shugo (provincial constable) strengthened the control of his territory and became Shugo daimyo (Japanese territorial lord as provincial constable).
In the Sengoku period (period of warring states), a large feudal lord who established a strong control over his territory appeared and was called a daimyo (Japanese territorial lord) or Sengoku daimyo (Japanese territorial lord in the Sengoku period).
In the Edo period, the term came to refer to samurai families which were given by the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) with more than 10,000 koku.
A samurai under the direct control of the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) was called Jikisan (a direct retainer of the bakufu) who was less than 10,000 koku (approximately 1.8 million liters of crop yield).
As daimyo (Japanese feudal lord) had a character of the feudal lord and the character of the character of the feudal lord in China, they were also referred to as daimyo lords in the same way as lords.
