Daimyo (Japanese feudal lord) in the Edo period
In the Edo period, the rank of the daimyo (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) was determined according to the social status, official court rank, Kokudaka (crop yield), official rank, and post.
First, according to the relationship with the Tokugawa Shogun family, it is classified as Oie-mon Gate (Chihan, Shinpan-daimyo), fudai daimyo (a daimyo in hereditary vassal to the Tokugawa family) who was a vassal of the Tokugawa family before the Battle of Sekigahara, and tozama daimyo (nonhereditary feudal lord) who became a vassal of the Tokugawa family from around the Battle of Sekigahara.
When the first Shogun family was discontinued, the Tokugawa gosanke (three privileged branches of the Tokugawa family) was established to maintain the bloodline of the bloodline, surveillance of the daimyo throughout the country, and to support the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), and the Tokugawa gosanke (three privileged branches of the Tokugawa family), which was allowed to use the Tokugawa family name of the Tokugawa family as well as the shogun family, and Yorinobu TOKUGAWA, the ninth son of the ninth son of Yorifusa TOKUGAWA, was appointed to the Mito Domain.
